            <tr><td>Immersion heater, unvented cylinder</td><td>£275</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Unvented cylinder yearly service</td><td>£120</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Repairs and fault finding</td><td>£120 first hour (weekdays 9am to 4pm)</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <h3>What is in the price?</h3>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>The new cylinder, supplied and fitted</li>
        <li>All pipe connections, valves and fittings</li>
        <li>Safety valves, expansion vessel and discharge pipe on unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>Testing and setting up before we leave</li>
        <li>Old cylinder drained, removed and taken away</li>
        <li>Building Regulations paperwork for unvented cylinders</li>
        <li>12-month workmanship guarantee</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Repairs out of hours are £150 for the first hour in the evening, at night and at weekends, and £180 on bank holidays. There is no call-out fee. If a job needs more time, we tell you the price before we carry on.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="loft">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Do I need a new cylinder for my Wirral loft conversion?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If the cold water tank in your loft feeds your hot water, yes. Once the tank goes, the old vented cylinder cannot work. An unvented cylinder fixes this.</p>
      <p>Loft conversions are common across the Wirral. Most builders want the loft tank out to free up headroom and floor space. That tank feeds a vented cylinder. Take it away, and the cylinder has no water supply.</p>
      <p>An unvented cylinder is fed straight from the mains. It needs no loft tank. You also get mains pressure hot water, so your showers get stronger.</p>
      <ul class="sg-ticks">
        <li>Change from vented to unvented from £1,895, including VAT</li>
        <li>Fitted by a G3 qualified engineer, as the law requires</li>
        <li>We check your mains pressure and flow before we quote</li>
        <li>We agree dates with your builder, so the work fits the project</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Tell us early in the project. The cylinder work is easier to plan before the new floor goes down.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="ensuite">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Will my cylinder cope with a new en-suite?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">A vented system often struggles once two showers run at once. A well-sized unvented cylinder gives mains pressure to every bathroom.</p>
      <p>We see this most in the larger detached and semi-detached homes in Heswall, Pensby and Bromborough. A family adds an en-suite to the main bedroom. Then the old loft tank system cannot keep up.</p>
      <p>With a vented system, the pressure comes from the height of the loft tank. Every tap shares it. Run two showers, and both go weak.</p>
      <p>An unvented cylinder runs at mains pressure. For a home with two bathrooms, we usually suggest 210 litres or more. We size it at the survey.</p>
      <p>Our <a href="/bathroom-fitters-wirral">bathroom fitters on the Wirral</a> can fit the en-suite too. You get one team and one fixed price for the lot.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="old">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Is it time to replace my old cylinder?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If your cylinder is over 20 years old, plan a swap before it fails. Most cylinders last 15 to 25 years.</p>
      <p>Many Victorian terraces in Birkenhead, Wallasey and Hoylake still have vented cylinders from the 1970s and 1980s. These are near or past the end of their life.</p>
      <p>An old copper cylinder can corrode from the inside. When it splits, it can flood a ceiling before you notice. That is worse if it goes overnight. A planned swap from £750 costs far less than the damage.</p>
      <div class="sg-cols">
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Often a repair</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>No hot water from the immersion heater</li>
            <li>Drip from a valve or fitting</li>
            <li>Banging or kettling from scale</li>
            <li>Water too hot or not hot enough</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
        <div class="sg-box">
          <h3>Usually a new cylinder</h3>
          <ul class="sg-ticks">
            <li>The tank itself is leaking or rusty</li>
            <li>You often run out of hot water</li>
            <li>It takes ages to heat back up</li>
            <li>The cylinder is 20 years old or more</li>
          </ul>
        </div>
      </div>
      <p style="margin-top:1rem;">We find the cause first and tell you honestly if a repair will do.</p>
      <div class="brx-cta">
        <p><strong>No hot water on the Wirral today?</strong> Call and we will tell you what we can do. Immersion heater swaps are often done the same day.</p>
        <a href={TEL} class="brx-btn">Call {PHONE}</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec brx-tint" id="types">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>Vented or unvented: which suits my Wirral home?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">If you keep your loft tank and are happy with your shower, a vented swap is the cheapest fix. For a loft conversion, an en-suite or stronger showers, go unvented.</p>
      <div class="brx-table-wrap">
        <table class="brx-table">
          <thead><tr><th>Compare</th><th>Vented cylinder</th><th>Unvented cylinder</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td><strong>Fed by</strong></td><td>A cold water tank in the loft</td><td>The mains water supply</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Pressure</strong></td><td>Low, from gravity</td><td>Mains pressure</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Loft tank</strong></td><td>Needed</td><td>Not needed for hot water</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Best for</strong></td><td>Older terraces with the tank in place</td><td>Loft conversions and homes with two bathrooms</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Who can fit it</strong></td><td>A qualified plumber</td><td>A G3 qualified engineer only</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Yearly service</strong></td><td>Not usually needed</td><td>Yes, to keep the warranty</td></tr>
            <tr><td><strong>Price with us</strong></td><td>from £750</td><td>from £1,450</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p>Most Wirral homes with a system or regular boiler have an indirect cylinder. The boiler heats it through a coil, with an immersion heater as a backup. All-electric homes may have a direct cylinder, heated by immersion only. We fit both.</p>
      <p>Only have one bathroom? A combi boiler may suit you better, with no tank at all. See our <a href="/boiler-installation-wirral">boiler installation prices on the Wirral</a>, from £2,195.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="brx-sec" id="how">
    <div class="brx-wrap">
      <h2>How does a cylinder installation work?</h2>
      <p class="brx-answer">We survey for free, give you a fixed written price, then fit the new cylinder. Most swaps are done in one day.</p>
      <ol class="sg-steps">
        <li><strong>Free survey.</strong> We check your space, your boiler and how much hot water you use. For unvented, we test your mains pressure and flow.</li>
        <li><strong>Fixed written price.</strong> It covers the cylinder, the fittings, the safety parts and the paperwork.</li>
        <li><strong>Fitting day.</strong> We protect your floors, drain the old cylinder and take it out. Then we fit and connect the new one.</li>
        <li><strong>Testing.</strong> We fill the system, check for leaks and test every safety valve. We show you the controls.</li>
        <li><strong>Paperwork.</strong> Unvented work is notified as the Building Regulations require. You get the certificate and warranty details.</li>
      </ol>
      <h3>Why G3 matters</h3>
      <p>An unvented cylinder holds hot water under mains pressure. Its safety valves must work every time. That is why only a qualified engineer can fit one. All our engineers are G3 qualified. Always ask for proof of G3 before anyone fits an unvented cylinder in your home.</p>
    </div>
  </section>
</ServiceGuide>
